The frame dump says where a draw call landed, not only what it drew.

The dump named *which* texture was drawn but not **where**, so a frame of
the original could not be compared with ours rectangle by rectangle. This
adds the bounds: the position is the first two floats of every vertex, so
a min/max over them gives the rectangle. It goes in at three entry points
— `DrawPrimitiveUP`, `DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP` and, most importantly,
`DrawPrimitive`/`DrawIndexedPrimitive`: BF2's interface goes through bound
vertex buffers, not through `*UP`, so without that path not one of the
frame's 853 calls gave any bounds.

Beside the rectangle it prints the vertex stride and the numbers of the
first and last vertex — after the position come the colour and the texture
coordinates, and those say which piece of a picture the game is showing.
And because the game folds small icons into a single call, where a batch is
a small multiple of four it prints the rectangle of every four vertices
too: those are the individual icons, which the shared bounding box says
nothing about.

`windows/.cargo/config.toml` is machine-specific — it points the linker at
this checkout's `reference/xwin` instead of `/opt/xwin`.

Applies to `reference/mtld3d` before `tools/mtld3d_build.sh`. Its companion
patches are `mtld3d_file_trigger.patch` and
`mtld3d_frame_dump_constants.patch`; the three are independent and can go
on in any order.

